  [BUG] drivers/perf/riscv_pmu_sbi.c:739-779  pmu_sbi_ctr_read
      Two separate non-atomic paths on CONFIG_32BIT: (a) Firmware counters issue independent SBI_EXT_PMU_COUNTER_FW_READ and SBI_EXT_PMU_COUNTER_FW_READ_HI ecalls with no atomicity guarantee—the firmware counter value can change between the two traps to M-mode. (b) Hardware counters read a lower CSR then CSR+0x80 (the *H variant) as two separate csr_read_num() calls with no retry loop. Both paths produce torn reads of 64-bit counters.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/include/asm/timex.h:71-81  get_cycles64
      Implements the canonical safe-read pattern (read upper, read lower, re-read upper, retry if upper changed) for CSR_TIME/CSR_TIMEH. However, on a trap-emulated implementation where the upper 32 bits are maintained by an M-mode trap handler on lower-half overflow, trap deferral (interrupts disabled, critical section, or higher-priority trap) means the re-read of CSR_TIMEH can return the pre-overflow value while CSR_TIME has already wrapped. The loop exits with hi1==hi2 but the 64-bit value is corrupted. The invariant the safe-read pattern depends on—that the upper word is updated synchronously with lower overflow—is violated.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/timex.h:51-54  get_cycles
      Reads only CSR_TIME (lower 32 bits on XLEN=32), returning a 32-bit truncated value typed as cycles_t (unsigned long). The function itself correctly reads the CSR it targets, but its API is semantically ambiguous on RV32: the name 'get_cycles' does not communicate that it returns only the lower half. Any caller that pairs this with a separate get_cycles_hi() call to reconstruct a 64-bit timestamp—without a retry loop—would get a torn read. The risk is in callers, not in this function itself, but the API invites misuse.
