Spec: Removed spec language that suggested implementing wide counters via trap handlers, because trap-emulated upper halves break the non-atomic safe-read sequence and yield silently corrupt counter snapshots.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_emulate_csr.c:59-101  sbi_emulate_csr_read
      On RV32, S-mode reads of CYCLE/INSTRET CSRs each trap independently to M-mode. The emulation reads CSR_MCYCLE (line 62) for the CYCLE trap and CSR_MCYCLEH (line 90) for the CYCLEH trap as two independent operations. S-mode software that reads CYCLE then CYCLEH (the only available sequence) can observe an inconsistent pair if the lower 32 bits overflow between the two traps. However, mcycleh is a hardware CSR (not a trap-maintained software word), so the specific spec condition ('trap-maintained software upper word' with deferred trap handler) does not directly apply. The implicit invariants are: (a) S-mode software must implement the recommended read-upper/read-lower/read-upper-compare sequence per RISC-V priv spec, and (b) the hardware provides a true 64-bit counter (mcycle+mcycleh) with atomic internal carry. If either invariant is violated, the emulation silently delivers a corrupted counter value.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_emulate_csr.c:125-135  sbi_emulate_csr_read
      HPM counter emulation for S/U-mode on RV32 uses the same non-atomic split-read pattern as the fixed counters. The switchcase_hpm macro (line 110) maps S-mode HPMCOUNTER3–HPMCOUNTER31 reads to csr_read(CSR_MHPMCOUNTERx), and the RV32 HPMCOUNTER3H–HPMCOUNTER31H reads (lines 131–134) independently map to csr_read(CSR_MHPMCOUNTERxH). Same vulnerability class as mcycle/minstret: S-mode reading HPMCOUNTER then HPMCOUNTERH observes two independent hardware snapshots with no carry-detection. Lower practical severity because S-mode typically uses SBI PMU (sbi_pmu_ctr_fw_read) rather than direct CSR access for HPM counters, and SBI PMU provides an atomic 64-bit read path.
  [RISK] include/sbi/riscv_asm.h:164-171  __csrr64 / csr_read64
      On RV32, __csrr64 (line 165) concatenates two independent 32-bit CSR reads: 'op(csr) | (uint64_t)op(csrh) << 32'. This is the foundational 64-bit CSR access primitive. Any caller using csr_read64() on RV32 inherently performs a non-atomic split access — the two individual csr_read() calls each have their own 'memory' clobber but nothing prevents hardware counter carry between them. This is fundamentally unfixable in software on RV32 without architectural extensions (Zacas or similar) since there are no 64-bit CSR instructions. The macro is correct for what it provides but inherently non-atomic for monotonically-incrementing counters. Code using csr_read64() on counter CSRs must explicitly handle the carry condition.
